------------------------------------------------------------\par
10.3 DATA GENERATION LOGIC\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
The synthetic data was designed to simulate realistic retail\par
business behaviour rather than generating completely random\par
independent observations.\par
\par
Relationships were established between:\par
\par
Customer \f2\u8594? Orders\par
\par
Product \u8594? Orders\par
\par
Customer Segment \u8594? Customer Behaviour\par
\par
Product \u8594? Category / Subcategory / Brand\par
\par
State \u8594? Region\par
\par
Order \u8594? Revenue / Cost / Profitability\par
\par
\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
10.4 CUSTOMER BEHAVIOUR DESIGN\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
Different customer segments were assigned different\par
behavioural characteristics.\par
\par
Consumer customers were designed to generally exhibit:\par
\par
- Smaller order values\par
- More variable purchasing frequency\par
\par
Small Business customers were designed to generally exhibit:\par
\par
- Moderate order values\par
- More regular purchasing behaviour\par
\par
Corporate customers were designed to generally exhibit:\par
\par
- Larger order values\par
- Higher purchasing frequency\par
\par
\par
These behavioural rules were introduced to create meaningful\par
variation for customer and segment profitability analysis.\par
\par
\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
10.5 PRODUCT AND PRICING LOGIC\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
Products were assigned:\par
\par
- Categories\par
- Subcategories\par
- Brands\par
- Unit prices\par
- Unit costs\par
\par
Order-level sales values were then generated using quantity\par
and product pricing.\par
\par
Gross Sales were derived from:\par
\par
Quantity \f3\'d7 Unit Price\par
\par
\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
10.6 DISCOUNT LOGIC\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
Discount-related variables were included to allow analysis\par
of the relationship between discounting and profitability.\par
\par
The dataset contains:\par
\par
- Discount\par
- Discount_Percent\par
- Discount_Amount\par
- Discount_Per_Unit\par
\par
Discounts affect the final revenue generated by a transaction\par
and therefore influence gross profit and gross margin.\par
\par
\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
10.7 REVENUE AND PROFITABILITY LOGIC\par
------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
The core financial relationships used in the dataset are:\par
\par
Gross Sales\par
\par
    = Quantity \'d7 Unit Price\par
\par
\par
Revenue\par
\par
    = Gross Sales \u8722? Discount Amount\par
\par
\par
COGS\par
\par
    = Quantity \'d7 Unit Cost\par
\par
\par
Gross Profit\par
\par
    = Revenue \u8722? COGS\par
\par
\par
Gross Margin\par
\par
